{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Background There is a significant increase in the number of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection reports in various countries. However, the trend of reinfection rate over time is not clear.",
      "reason": "The review’s rationale includes an existing-knowledge gap (reinfection reports increasing, but the time trend of reinfection rate is not clear), but it does not comprehensively describe the current state of knowledge and “important uncertainties” beyond this point."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "However, the trend of reinfection rate over time is not clear. Methods We searched PubMed, Web of Science, Medline, Embase, Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials, China National Knowledge Infrastructure, and Wanfang for cohort studies, case-control studies, and cross-sectional studies up to March 16, 2023, to conduct a meta-analysis of global SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rate.",
      "reason": "The manuscript states the need to address uncertainty in the time trend of reinfection rate and directly links it to conducting the meta-analysis (i.e., explaining the importance/need for the review)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "This study also indicated that the reinfection rate varies among different countries, such as Brazil, higher than other countries, with a reinfection rate of 6.60% (95% CI : 3.94 -10.84%), which migtht be influenced by different countries' epidemic prevention policies, susceptible populations, ecological environment, etc. [34]. The search strategy we used was based on previous studies... In addition, the advantage of this study was that compared with the previous meta-analysis of the SARSCoV-2 reinfection rate, first, to avoid the heterogeneous impact of SARSCoV-2 reinfection definition on SARSCoV-2 reinfection rates...",
      "reason": "The authors note a comparison/advantage relative to a previous meta-analysis, implying related prior evidence exists and motivating the current approach; however, the evidence provided does not show an explicit explanation with a specific citation for the previous review (or a clear update/replication rationale meeting the criterion as written)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The provided evidence describes a meta-analysis/meta-regression of reinfection rates and does not describe an intervention or mechanism of action."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The provided evidence does not describe a complex intervention or delivery context that would require a logic model."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 64
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Therefore, the aim of this study was to conduct a meta-analysis to describe and summarize the current reinfection rates in different regions and among diverse populations. Furthermore, the study sought to examine trends in reinfection rates over time",
      "reason": "The review objective(s) are explicitly stated as aims to (1) describe/summarize current reinfection rates by region/population and (2) examine trends in reinfection rates over time."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "A meta-regression analysis was conducted on literature reporting cases of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection globally to estimate the incidence of reinfection of SARS-CoV-2",
      "reason": "This is a meta-regression/meta-analysis to estimate incidence/rates of reinfection (an observational risk/epidemiologic question), not a review of intervention effects with planned intervention comparisons stated using PICO."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 78
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The inclusion criteria were: (1) Conformed to research questions, i.e., studies that estimated the reinfection rate of SARS-CoV-2 or we could calculate it based on the data provided in the paper; (2) The study design included cohort study, case-control study, and descriptive study; (3) The study fited our working definition of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection. ... reinfection working definition in this study was based on a positive laboratory result at least 90 days after laboratory confirmation of primary infection",
      "reason": "Eligibility for study characteristics is specified for the research question, eligible study designs, and a reinfection definition with a required interval (≥90 days), but settings and explicit follow-up requirements (beyond the interval definition) are not clearly specified."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "To avoid language bias, the search was not limited to any language. We conducted preliminary screening based on the English title and English abstract provided by nonEnglish articles. ... There were no non-English articles in the final articles included in this study.",
      "reason": "Language restriction/handling is described (search not limited to any language; translation for screening), but dissemination year, publication/report status, and other report-characteristic eligibility criteria are not specified in the retrieved evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The retrieved evidence does not state that exclusions were distinguished based on whether outcomes were not measured versus whether outcome results were not reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Subgroup analyses were performed by meta-regression to fit stratified rates of reinfection based on variables such as variant and country, and we also used spline regression to fit time-varying reinfection rates that did not account for heterogeneity.",
      "reason": "The review describes grouping variables used for stratified analyses (e.g., variant, country) and methods for time-varying estimation, but the retrieved evidence does not clearly link these groups to specific comparison statements/objectives."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "To avoid language bias, the search was not limited to any language.",
      "reason": "A rationale is provided for a notable eligibility-related restriction/approach (not limiting by language to avoid language bias)."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 72
}

